Restrictions and Limitations
The principal limitations of the results obtainable by mercury porosimetry are the following:
● The extent of the pore volume filled with mercury is limited by a maximum pressure. In such conditions mercury cannot penetrate into very small pores. ● Contrary to the former, the penetration into very large pores is limited by the height of the sample, which determines a minimum pressure. Consequently, very large pores may remain unfilled. ● In the process of porosity measurement, mercury not only penetrates into pores, but it also fills the space between particles that distorts the results obtained for the true volume of coarse pores.
Detailed discussion about restrictions and limitations of mercury porosimetry can be found in a series of works [26, 27].
